sections.body:
Good process management needs **capacity** (expertise and resources) inside a **sound project governance** structure, wrapped in a clear **framework**. Many PPP failures come from challenges that can only be handled with proper governance. The Guide lists what that governance should include:

- sufficient resources in the project team (not too large, not too small), a **full-time dedicated project manager** and suitable advisors;
- a clearly identified **Project Owner** and **Project Champion**;
- clear **accountability**;
- an **advocate** for the project outside the project team;
- a clear **decision framework** (project boards linked to programme governance) and decision chain;
- proper **stakeholder management** from the early stages;
- fluent and clear **communication**, including to the general public.

A sound framework and a **strategic, programmatic** approach not only reduce failures; the Guide says it is the **only way to maximise VfM**. Governments themselves must be ready to control pitfalls. A PPP framework should generally be in place **before** a PPP programme is announced, although it can be adapted as experience grows. Launching **pathfinder projects** to test the market is good practice when the market is not yet mature.

Figure 1.9 brings this together: projects must be sensible, suitable, feasible and properly structured and tendered; there must be sound project governance and a sound framework; **market appetite** (prospective bidders) depends on good projects, reliable governance and country factors; and both governance and framework must adapt to market and country limits.
